==English==

===Etymology===
Origin uncertain; perhaps related to Old French [[gogue]] ‘game’, or from English [[gaw]].

===Pronunciation===
*{{IPA|/ˈgju:gɔ:/}} ''(UK)''
*{{audio|en-us-gewgaw.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[showy]] [[trifle]], a toy; a showy ornament or decoration
#: ''“A heavy '''gewgaw''' called a crown.”'' &mdash;Dryden.


Bidders paid hundreds of thousands of dollars for worthless gewgaws--fake pearls, ashtrays, golf clubs--merely, one supposes, because they were touched by the hand of this celebrity of celebrities.

===Adjective===
'''gewgaw'''

# [[showy|Showy]]; [[unreal]]; [[pretentious]].
#: ''Seeing his '''gewgaw''' castle shine.'' &mdash;Tennyson.

